## Module 4 — Attachment-Based Delivery & Hybrid Impersonation {#module-4}

### 4.1  Advanced Phishing Techniques

- **Attachment-Based Delivery:** Shifting the initial exploitation vector away from embedded hyperlinks toward a file attachment (PDF or HTML). This bypasses Secure Email Gateways (SEGs) that primarily inspect and block known malicious URLs within the email body text.

- **Hybrid Impersonation (Trust Building):** Mixing malicious indicators with references to entirely legitimate infrastructure. By including official domains (e.g., `help.netflix.com`), attackers confuse both the victim and automated reputation-scoring filters.

- **Visual Typosquatting:** Intentionally misspelling a well-known brand name in the presentation headers (e.g., `Netllx` using two lowercase "l"s instead of "fl"). This exploits the human brain's tendency to skim text and read what it expects rather than the literal characters on screen.

- **Psychological Pressure (Account Suspension):** Manufacturing an artificial administrative crisis to induce fear and compel immediate compliance, overriding standard security instincts.

---

### 4.2  Multi-Stage Exploitation Path

```
[Inbound Email]
   │  Bypasses link filters — contains no direct malicious hyperlinks
   ▼
[PDF / HTML Attachment]
   │  User opens file locally, escaping the monitored email environment
   ▼
[Embedded Hyperlink Inside Attachment]
   │  "Update Payment Account" button launches malicious external URL
   ▼
[Credential / Payment Trap]
   │  User inputs sensitive billing data on a fake domain
   ▼
[Attacker Server]  ▶  Data exfiltrated.
```

---

### 4.3  Key Indicators of Compromise (IOCs)

- **Header / Display Name Mismatch:** The sender name displays a branded department ("Netllx billing") while the underlying transport routing address points to a completely unrelated external domain.

- **Atypical Communication Formats:** Non-standard support information — such as an oddly formatted phone number — deviates from standard corporate automated billing templates.

- **Local File Context Shift:** The email itself contains no immediate threat action; it only instructs the user to open a document. Once opened, the security context shifts from the monitored email client to a local PDF reader, where URL hovering and tracking controls may be weaker.

---

### 4.4  SOC Reference Summary

| Vector / Element | Adversary Objective | Analyst Mitigation / Action |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Visual Typosquatting** (`Netllx`) | Bypass keyword filters & deceive user inspection | Flag string anomalies using regex or YARA rules. |
| **File Attachment (PDF)** | Hide malicious URLs from inbound email scanners | Extract file in an isolated sandbox; parse embedded objects. |
| **Mixed Legitimate Domains** | Neutralize reputation scoring engines | Analyze all outbound connections from the attachment, ignoring safe domains. |

